course: dictionary
lesson: 12804
title: Elicit: drawing out a reaction
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: To elicit something means to draw it out of someone, usually by asking a question or doing something. It is a verb: elicit, elicits, elicited, eliciting. «The teacher asked a clever question to elicit ideas from the class.» The question pulls the ideas out of the children.
explain: You can elicit an answer, a smile, a laugh or information. «The funny clown elicited a loud laugh from the crowd.» The clown did something, and the laugh came back because of it. Something happens, and a reaction comes out.
explain: To check the meaning, ask: what did someone do, and what came back? Then swap in a simpler phrase such as "got out of" or "drew out". If the sentence still makes sense, you have found the meaning.
fact: Elicit means to get a reaction, answer or information from someone.
fact: You often elicit something by asking questions or doing something.
fact: Common phrases are «elicit a response», «elicit information» and «elicit a smile».
fact: In science, a stimulus elicits a response.
fact: Elicit is a verb, so it changes: elicits, elicited, eliciting.
exercise: «The teacher asked a question to elicit ideas from the class.» What does elicit mean here? | To write ideas on the wall | To hide ideas | To throw away ideas | *To draw out ideas
exercise: «The joke elicited a loud laugh.» Which phrase means the same as elicited a laugh? | Stopped the laugh | Hid the laughing face | Forgot the laugh | *Got a laugh out of people
exercise: «Her smile elicited a smile from her friend.» Which word is the verb in this sentence? | Smile | Her | Friend | *Elicited
test: «The scientist showed a bright light to elicit a response from the mouse.» What does elicit a response mean? | To paint the light | To lose the mouse | To go to sleep | *To get a reaction from the mouse
test: Which sentence uses elicit correctly? | The cat elicited a long nap. | *The question elicited a helpful answer from the class. | I elicited my bag to school. | The rain elicited the sofa.
exam: Which phrase best matches elicit information? | Read a book quietly | *Ask questions to get facts | Forget what you heard | Hide all the facts
